Artificial red berries and green leaves extend along a flexible length of approximately 1.7 m. The stems follow the line of a banister or mantel, giving you a way to introduce a bright berry colour across a whole display. It can be used indoors or outdoors, so the same decoration can move between a hallway and an entrance as your plans change.

A garland lets you carry one theme across a length of the room. It can pick up the line of a shelf, a stair rail or a doorway, tying together different parts of a display without needing several separate decorations at each point.

On a staircase, plan the fixing points so the handrail stays easy to grip. Follow the line of the stairs from below as you arrange the length, checking that the lowest sections stay clear of the steps. For a dining table, try the garland along the centre before setting out plates. A shallow curve at either end keeps it within the table outline while leaving the middle available for a low seasonal display.

Use your own ribbon at intervals to introduce a second colour without covering the main decorative detail. A shallow drape uses less of the available length and gives the greenery a cleaner line along the edge of a shelf.

Measure the route you want the garland to follow, including the curves or dips along the way. A length laid straight across a mantel reaches further than the same length arranged in a series of deep swags. Viewed from across the room, a few gentle curves usually show the design more clearly than many tight bends. Lay the garland along its route before securing it and check how the ends fall from the main display.

When you take the display down, release each fixing in turn and support the loose length as you go. Store it in broad curves, with enough space around the decorative details to avoid flattening them.
